: > Step 7.4.\n369: \n370: > [!IMPORTANT]\n371: >\n372: > **Fallback:** If `gcloud trigger` fails, use the bundled script: Run script\n373: > with -- help to discover and learn the interface.\n374: >\n375: > ```\n376: > python scripts/trigger/airflow_trigger.py \\ --project <PROJECT_ID>\n377: > --location <REGION> \\ --environment <COMPOSER_ENV> --dag_id <PIPELINE_ID>\n378: > ```\n379: >\n380: > Get `project`, `region`, and `composer_environment` from `deployment.yaml`.\n381: \n382: ## Definition of done\n383: \n384: -   `deployment.yaml` file is created successfully.\n385: -   The orchestration pipeline file (e.g., `orchestration_pipeline.yaml`) is\n386:     created successfully, includes a mandatory `endTime` for every schedule, and\n387:     passes the validation command: `gcloud beta orchestration-pipelines validate\n388:     --environment=<ENV_NAME>`\n389: -   If user requested to **deploy** the orchestration pipeline, the `gcloud beta\n390:     orchestration-pipelines deploy --environment=<ENV_NAME> --local` command\n391:     should return a success message with a version/bundle ID.\n392: -   If user requested to **trigger/run** the orchestration pipeline:\n393:     1.  Deploy succeeded (bundle ID extracted from output)\n394:     2.  DAG appeared in `gcloud beta orchestration-pipelines list` within 2 min\n395:     3.  `gcloud beta orchestration-pipelines trigger` returned success\n396:     4.  Run is visible in `gcloud beta orchestration-pipelines runs list`\n397: \n398: ## Other actions\n399: \n400: If requested to pause/stop the orchestration pipeline, use\n401: \n402: ```bash\n403:     # Replace <ENV_NAME>, <BUNDLE_ID>, <PIPELINE_ID> with actual values\n404:     gcloud beta orchestration-pipelines pause \\\n405:     --environment=<ENV_NAME> \\\n406:     --bundle=<BUNDLE_ID> \\\n407:     --pipeline=<PIPELINE_ID>\n408: ```\n409: \n410: If requested to unpause/resume the orchestration pipeline, use\n411: \n412: ```bash\n413:     # Replace <ENV_NAME>, <BUNDLE_ID>, <PIPELINE_ID> with actual values\n414:     gcloud beta orchestration-pipelines unpause \\\n415:     --environment=<ENV_NAME> \\\n416:     --bundle=<BUNDLE_ID> \\\n417:     --pipeline=<PIPELINE_ID>\n418: ```\n419: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":13,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T23:26:09Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-24T04:56:09+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-24T04:56:09+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/gcp-pipeline-resource-provisioning/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 172\nTotal Bytes: 6528\nShowing lines 1 to 172\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: name: gcp-pipeline-resource-provisioning\n3: description: |\n4:   Automates declarative resource creation and provisioning for data pipelines, supporting BigQuery, Dataform, Dataproc, BigQuery Data Transfer Service (DTS), and other resources. It manages environment-specific configurations (dev, staging, prod) through a deployment.yaml file.\n5:   Use when:\n6:   - Modifying or creating deployment.yaml for deployment settings.\n7:   - Resolving environment-specific variables (e.g., Project IDs, Regions) for deployment.\n8:   - Provisioning supported infrastructure like BigQuery datasets/tables, Dataform resources, or DTS resources via deployment.yaml.\n9:   Do not use when:\n10:   - Resources already exist.\n11:   - Managing resources not supported by `gcloud beta orchestration-pipelines resource-types list`.\n12:   - Managing general cloud infrastructure (VMs, networks, Kubernetes, IAM policies), which are better suited for Terraform.\n13:   - Infrastructure spans multiple cloud providers (AWS, Azure, etc.).\n14:   - Already uses Terraform for the target resources.\n15: license: Apache-2.0\n16: metadata:\n17:   version: v1\n18:   publisher: google\n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## How to use this skill\n22: \n23: Create or update existing `deployment.yaml` file and deploy resources. All\n24: configuration files MUST be maintained together in the repository root.\n25: \n26: ### Mandatory labels\n27: \n28: > [!IMPORTANT]\n29: >\n30: > Whenever you generate resource definitions in `deployment.yaml`, you **MUST**\n31: > directly populate the `datacloud` label under `definition.labels` for every\n32: > resource to track the source of creation. Determine the value based on your\n33: > current IDE environment:\n34: >\n35: > -   For Antigravity, set `datacloud: \"antigravity\"`\n36: > -   For VS Code, set `datacloud: \"vscode\"`\n37: > -   For any other environment, set `datacloud: \"other\"`\n38: >\n39: > Do not use a variable substitution for this label; hardcode the appropriate\n40: > string value directly into each resource definition (e.g., replacing\n41: > `__REQUIRED_LABEL__` placeholders).\n42: >\n43: > **Special rule for BigQuery DTS Ingestion:** Whenever you generate a\n44: > `bigquerydatatransfer.transferConfig` in `deployment.yaml`, you **MUST** also\n45: > explicitly define its target destination `bigquery.dataset` in the same file\n46: > and apply the `datacloud` label to it. You must do this even if the dataset\n47: > already exists, to ensure the destination dataset's labels are patched and\n48: > updated.\n49: \n50: ### Step 1: Supported Resource Types\n51: \n52: The framework supports deploying various GCP resources. To see the comprehensive\n53: list of supported resource types, run the following command:\n54: \n55: ```\n56: gcloud beta orchestration-pipelines resource-types list\n57: ```\n58: \n59: Refer to: references/gcp-pipeline-resource-provisioning_spec.md to understand\n60: the template for `deployment.yaml`.\n61: \n62: ### Step 2: Discover Environment Parameters\n63: \n64: Before generating configurations, discover the actual values for the target\n65: project, region, environment, and commit SHA.\n66: \n67: > [!TIP]\n68: >\n69: > If `deployment.yaml` already exists in the repository root, prioritize\n70: > extracting `project` and `region` from the target environment configuration\n71: > (e.g., `dev`).\n72: \n73: 1.  **Project ID**:\n74: \n75:     ```bash\n76:     gcloud config get project\n77:     ```\n78: \n79: 2.  **Project Number**:\n80: \n81:     ```bash\n82:     gcloud projects describe $(gcloud config get project) --format=\"value(projectNumber)\"\n83:     ```\n84: \n85: 3.  **Region**:\n86: \n87:     ```bash\n88:     gcloud config get-value compute/region\n89:     ```\n90: \n91: 4.  **Commit SHA**:\n92: \n93:     ```bash\n94:     git rev-parse HEAD\n95:     ```\n96: \n97: 5.  **Environment Name**: If initialization is needed, you MUST ask the user for\n98:     the environment name. If the user does not provide it, use **dev** as the\n99:     default.\n100: \n101: > [!TIP]\n102: >\n103: > Use these commands to replace placeholders like `YOUR_PROJECT_ID` with actual\n104: > values. Always remove associated comments that start with TODO once replaced.\n105: \n106: ### Step 3: Generate or update deployment.yaml\n107: \n108: Create or update `deployment.yaml` in the repository root. This file maps\n109: supported environments (**dev**, **stage**, **prod**) to their specific\n110: configurations and resources.\n111: \n112: > [!TIP]\n113: >\n114: > **Use the Reference Spec**: The agent can use the\n115: > **`references/gcp_pipeline_resource_provisioning_spec.md`** file as a\n116: > template. It includes sample definitions for select supported resource types.\n117: > Copy and adapt the required resource blocks into the `deployment.yaml`. Use\n118: > `gcloud beta orchestration-pipelines resource-types list` when needed.\n119: \n120: > [!IMPORTANT]\n121: >\n122: > **Handling Secrets & Privacy (CRITICAL)**: NEVER hardcode plain-text secrets\n123: > in `deployment.yaml`.\n124: >\n125: > -   **Sensitive Data (Secrets):** Sensitive information such as passwords, API\n126: >     keys, and other sensitive information MUST be stored in Secret Manager and\n127: >     declared in the `secrets:` block of `deployment.yaml`.\n128: > -   **Non-Sensitive Data (Variables):** General configuration (e.g., dataset\n129: >     names, table IDs, regions) could be declared in the `variables:` block.\n130: > -   **Substitution via `{{ VAR }}`:** Both `variables:` and `secrets:` MUST be\n131: >     used as `{{ VARIABLE_NAME }}` substitutions in resource definitions.\n132: > -   **No Creation**: The agent MUST NOT use the framework to *create* new\n133: >     secrets. If `gcloud` indicates the secret does not exist, the agent MUST\n134: >     ask the user to create it manually and then re-verify.\n135: > -   **Reference Only Policy**: The agent's role is strictly limited to\n136: >     *referencing* existing secrets. The agent MUST NEVER read, print, or\n137: >     inspect the values of secrets.\n138: > -   **Safe Deployment**: The actual value injection happens during deployment\n139: >     execution. The agent only provides the reference.\n140: > -   **Manual Secret Management**: Advise the user to manage secret payloads\n141: >     and versions manually.\n142: \n143: ### Step 4: Validation\n144: \n145: The agent MUST validate the `deployment.yaml` before generating the deployment\n146: script. This ensures the configuration is syntactically correct and all\n147: variables are resolvable.\n148: \n149: ```\n150: gcloud beta orchestration-pipelines validate --environment=<ENV_NAME>\n151: ```\n152: \n153: ### Step 5: Deployment\n154: \n155: Run the following command to deploy the resources to the target environment.\n156: \n157: ```\n158: gcloud beta orchestration-pipelines deploy --environment=<ENV_NAME> --local\n159: ```\n160: \n161: > [!NOTE]\n162: >\n163: > If a new transfer is being created, make sure to NOT remove the DTS transfer\n164: > resource from `deployment.yaml` after it completes the run.\n165: \n166: ## Definition of Done\n167: \n168: -   `deployment.yaml` exists in the repository root with actual discovered\n169:     values (no placeholders) and correct resource definitions.\n170: -   The agent runs the deployment command to perform the deployment, and it\n171:     executes successfully (exit code 0).\n172: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":14,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T23:26:09Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-24T04:56:09+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-24T04:56:09+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/gcp-spark/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 105\nTotal Bytes: 4787\nShowing lines 1 to 105\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: name: gcp-spark\n3: description: |\n4:   Develops and executes Spark code on Dataproc Clusters and Serverless.\n5:   Reads and writes data using BigLake Iceberg catalogs, BigQuery and Spanner.\n6:   Debugs execution failures.\n7:   Use when:\n8:   - Writing Spark ETL pipelines on GCP.\n9:   - Training or running inference with ML models with spark on GCP.\n10:   - Managing Spark clusters, jobs, batches, and interactive sessions.\n11:   Don't use when:\n12:   - Writing generic Python scripts that don't use Spark.\n13:   - Performing simple SQL queries that can be done directly in BigQuery.\n14: license: Apache-2.0\n15: metadata:\n16:   version: v4\n17:   publisher: google\n18: ---\n19: \n20: # Spark on Dataproc\n21: \n22: > [!IMPORTANT]\n23: >\n24: > You MUST ALWAYS follow the Task Execution Workflow when writing spark code.\n25: \n26: ## Task Execution Workflow\n27: \n28: 1.  **Understand schemas**: **ALWAYS** use `@skill:discovering-gcp-data-assets`\n29:     skill or `references/schema_direct_inspection.md` to understand input and\n30:     output schemas. Include the schema in your thought process BEFORE generating\n31:     any code. Do NOT guess column names. Unless explicitly specified, assume\n32:     that the assets are located in the same project. Avoid scanning for assets\n33:     across other projects as it can take a long time.\n34: 2.  **Verify source accessibility**: verify access/existence using `gcloud\n35:     storage ls gs://<path-to-dataset>`. If accessing or reading a GCS path fails\n36:     with a storage error e.g., permission errors like `403\n37:     Forbidden`/`Forbidden`/`PermissionDenied`, or location errors like `404 Not\n38:     Found`/`NotFound`/`FileNotFoundException` you should report the error\n39:     immediately. Either (1) ask the user what to do next, or (2) if asked to\n40:     execute a notebook, save the notebook with the error output and recommend\n41:     next steps to resolve the issue. Do NOT scan all buckets for alternative\n42:     fallback datasets when encountering GCS errors.\n43: 3.  **Generate spark code**:\n44:     *   **Output Format**: **ALWAYS** generate code in **Python Notebooks\n45:         (.ipynb)** format. Generate scripts (.py) only if explicitly requested.\n46:     *   **Read and Write data**: **ALWAYS** Refer to\n47:         `references/read_write_data.md` when reading or writing data.\n48:     *   **ML Tasks**: Refer to `@skill:ml-best-practices` skill and\n49:         `references/ml_tasks.md` when generating ML code.\n50:     *   **Spark Optimizations**: **ALWAYS** refer to\n51:         `references/spark_optimizations.md` when generating spark code and apply\n52:         optimization whenever applicable.\n53: 4.  **Verify schema before write**: **ALWAYS** verify that the dataframe and\n54:     destination schema match, use `df.printSchema()` for dataframe schema and\n55:     refer to `@skill:discovering-gcp-data-assets` skill or\n56:     `references/schema_direct_inspection.md` to verify destination schema.\n57: 5.  **Compile code before executing**: For notebooks convert them to python\n58:     script using `jupyter nbconvert --to script your-notebook.ipynb` first. Then\n59:     compile the resulting python script using `python3 -m py_compile\n60:     your-script.py`. The same can be done for pyspark source code.\n61: 6.  **Execute script**: When requested to run a job, script, session refer to\n62:     `references/gcloud_dataproc.md` on how to execute generated code on Managed\n63:     Spark. This DOES NOT apply when generating notebooks.\n64: \n65: --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n66: \n67: ## Common Mistakes Checklist\n68: \n69: > [!CAUTION]\n70: >\n71: > Ensure you verify this checklist to avoid mistakes\n72: \n73: Before submitting a job, verify:\n74: \n75: -   [ ] **All imports present** (`col`, `when`, `lit`, etc. from\n76:     `pyspark.sql.functions`)\n77: -   [ ] **`vector_to_array` from correct module** use `from pyspark.ml.functions\n78:     import vector_to_array` (NOT `pyspark.sql.functions`)\n79: -   [ ] **DataFrame schema matches target Iceberg table** verify with\n80:     `df.printSchema()` before writing\n81: -   [ ] **CSV files read with `header` and `inferSchema`** without these, the\n82:     header row becomes data and all columns are strings\n83: -   [ ] **Avoid toPandas()** Converting a pyspark dataframe to pandas by calling\n84:     toPandas() can lead to out of memory errors. Only acceptable for building\n85:     visualizations in Spark 3.5\n86: \n87: --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n88: \n89: ## IAM Requirements\n90: \n91: The Dataproc service account needs:\n92: \n93: *   `roles/dataproc.worker`: Job execution\n94: *   `roles/biglake.admin`: Iceberg table management\n95: *   `roles/bigquery.jobUser`: Query materialization\n96: *   `roles/storage.objectUser`: Read/write GCS\n97: *   `roles/spanner.databaseUser`: Spanner writes\n98: \n99: --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n100: \n101: ## Spark resource management\n102: \n103: Refer to `references/gcloud_dataproc.md` for detailed guidelines on managing\n104: Spark clusters, jobs, batches, and interactive sessions.\n105: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":15,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T23:26:09Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-24T04:56:09+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-24T04:56:09+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/gcs-security-assessment/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 96\nTotal Bytes: 6817\nShowing lines 1 to 96\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: name: gcs-security-assessment\n3: description: 'Assesses security posture, evaluates risks, and checks SAIF compliance\n4:   for Google Cloud Storage buckets or projects. Use when the user requests security\n5:   scans, vulnerability checks, or SAIF assessments. Don''t use when: The user is asking\n6:   about non-GCS resources (Compute Engine, GKE, etc.), investigating a live production\n7:   outage, or asking general security questions not tied to a specific project or bucket.'\n8: license: Apache-2.0\n9: metadata:\n10:   version: v2\n11:   publisher: google\n12:   tags:\n13:     - gcs\n14:     - security\n15:     - compliance\n16:     - saif\n17:   category: security\n18:   support_tier: primary\n19: ---\n20: \n21: # Security Posture Assessment Skill\n22: \n23: You are a Google Cloud Storage security assessment agent trained on Google's\n24: [Secure AI Framework (SAIF)](https://saif.google/secure-ai-framework/saif-map).\n25: Your job is to evaluate GCS bucket and project configurations, identify **toxic\n26: combinations** of vulnerabilities, and provide actionable remediation.\n27: \n28: > [!IMPORTANT]\n29: >\n30: > You are NOT a generic security chatbot. You MUST ground every finding in\n31: > telemetry signals you have actually gathered. NEVER hallucinate findings or\n32: > assume configurations you have not verified. If you cannot gather a signal,\n33: > say so explicitly and skip that check.\n34: \n35: > [!CAUTION]\n36: >\n37: > **CRITICAL: Never execute destructive commands (e.g., rm, rb, IAM policy\n38: > changes) without first printing the exact command and explicitly asking the\n39: > user for a Y/N confirmation.**\n40: \n41: ## Philosophy\n42: \n43: Traditional security tools generate isolated alerts from static rules (e.g.,\n44: \"bucket is public\"). You correlate multiple signals to detect **toxic\n45: combinations** — scenarios where individually low-risk configurations combine to\n46: create critical exposures. A public bucket storing marketing PDFs is very\n47: different from a public bucket storing ML training data with no CMEK, no VPC-SC,\n48: and no audit logging.\n49: \n50: ## Phase Summary Table\n51: \n52: Phase                             | Inputs                                   | Outputs                               | Reference\n53: :-------------------------------- | :--------------------------------------- | :------------------------------------ | :--------\n54: **1. Discover Scope & Telemetry** | User input (Project ID/Buckets/Datasets) | Scope confirmation, Telemetry signals | `references/phases/discover.md`\n55: **2. Bucket Classification**      | Telemetry signals                        | Bucket classifications                | `references/phases/classification.md`\n56: **3. Baseline Security Eval**     | Telemetry signals, Classifications       | Baseline failures                     | `references/phases/baseline.md`\n57: **4. Toxic Combo Analysis**       | Telemetry signals, Classifications       | Toxic combination findings            | `references/phases/toxic_analysis.md`\n58: **5. Output**                     | Findings from all phases                 | Formatted assessment report           | `references/phases/output.md`\n59: \n60: ## Workflow Execution\n61: \n62: When invoked, the agent **MUST follow this exact sequence**:\n63: \n64: 1.  **Start at Phase 1**: Discover scope and gather telemetry. Use the\n65:     referenced file for decisions. **CRITICAL: If multiple Storage Insights\n66:     datasets are discovered, you MUST STOP and ASK the user to select one. Do\n67:     NOT auto-select a dataset or proceed with an assumed one.** Do not assume\n68:     anything before reading the steps referenced in the phase itself.\n69: 2.  **Do not skip phases**: You must complete Phase N before proceeding to Phase\n70:     N+1.\n71: 3.  **Strict adherence**: Follow all steps defined in each phase. Do not\n72:     optimize or deviate.\n73: 4.  **Gating & analysis scope**: Only a failed **required** preflight check\n74:     (`adc`) sets `ready_to_proceed` to `false` — when it does you **MUST STOP\n75:     IMMEDIATELY**, do NOT invoke any telemetry script, and report the fix.\n76:     Otherwise the preflight's `analysis_scope` field — NOT `ready_to_proceed` —\n77:     selects depth: `full` (run everything) or `project_only` (Storage Insights\n78:     unavailable — do NOT bail out; run a project-level assessment with ONLY\n79:     `evaluate_project_security_posture.py`, do NOT run the SI-backed\n80:     `fetch_bucket_telemetry.py` / `fetch_object_telemetry.py`, and recommend\n81:     SI). Phase 1 (`discover.md`) defines exactly how to branch.\n82: \n83: ## Error Handling\n84: \n85: Problem                             | Cause                                                                       | Fix\n86: ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---\n87: PermissionDenied on VPC-SC check    | Caller lacks `accesscontextmanager.policies.list`                           | Inform user. Mark VPC-SC status as UNKNOWN and use that wording **consistently across every section of the report** — Section 2, Section 3, narrative summaries, key findings, fixes. Do NOT assume the perimeter is configured AND do NOT assume it is missing, lacking, or \"not enforced\" — neither inference is supported by an unavailable signal.\n88: PermissionDenied on IAM Recommender | Caller lacks `recommender.iamPolicyRecommendations.list`                    | Fall back to manual IAM policy inspection. Flag over-broad roles like `roles/storage.admin` and `roles/storage.objectAdmin`.\n89: Model Armor API not enabled         | `modelarmor.googleapis.com` not in services list                            | This IS a finding (not an error). Flag it as \"Model Armor not enabled\" in your assessment.\n90: Storage Insights API not enabled    | `storageinsights.googleapis.com` not enabled on the project                 | **DO NOT STOP.** `analysis_scope` is `project_only`; run the project-level assessment and relay the recommended check's `fix`. See `discover.md`.\n91: No SI dataset available             | SI is enabled but no dataset config exists, or wrong dataset name supplied  | **DO NOT STOP.** `analysis_scope` is `project_only`; run the project-level assessment and relay the `bigquery_dataset_access` check's `fix`. See `discover.md`.\n92: BQ MCP Server returns empty results | No buckets in project or wrong project                                      | Confirm project ID with user. If correct and empty, report \"No buckets found.\"\n93: Data Access audit logs check fails  | Caller lacks `resourcemanager.projects.getIamPolicy`                        | Inform user. Note that audit log status is unknown.\n94: Bucket has no tags or labels        | No SDP scan, no customer tags                                               | This is the \"Unclassified\" state. Treat as potentially sensitive. Recommend SDP.\n95: Output too verbose                  | Reasoning sections are too long, or shared remediations repeated per bucket | Condense reasoning to 2-3 sentences. Move shared remediations to Cross-Cutting Recommendations. If output exceeds ~80 lines, you are being too verbose.\n96: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
